From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ankur Kumar <ankit@bodygram.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 00:05:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] iio: hid-sensor-trigger: do not sleep on a failed poll
 interval read

_hid_sensor_power_state() reads the report interval before powering a
sensor up and then sleeps for two intervals. hid_sensor_read_poll_value()
returns -EINVAL when the read fails or the value is negative, and the
check only tests for non-zero, so msleep_interruptible() gets -22 * 2 as
an unsigned int: about 49.7 days.

The sensor hub of the MSI Prestige N16 Flip AI+ (MSFW1111, HID over SPI)
reports the interval of a powered-off sensor as 0xffffffff until the host
sets one, so after boot or a hub reset the first runtime resume of its
accelerometer hung the caller (an IIO buffer enable or a sysfs raw read)
in an interruptible sleep, and every other user of the device waited
behind it in rpm_resume(). Only sleep for a positive interval.
---
 drivers/iio/common/hid-sensors/hid-sensor-trigger.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/iio/common/hid-sensors/hid-sensor-trigger.c b/drivers/iio/common/hid-sensors/hid-sensor-trigger.c
--- a/drivers/iio/common/hid-sensors/hid-sensor-trigger.c
+++ b/drivers/iio/common/hid-sensors/hid-sensor-trigger.c
@@ -143,7 +143,7 @@ static int _hid_sensor_power_state(struct hid_sensor_common *st, bool state)
 	sensor_hub_get_feature(st->hsdev, st->power_state.report_id,
 			       st->power_state.index,
 			       sizeof(state_val), &state_val);
-	if (state && poll_value)
+	if (state && poll_value > 0)
 		msleep_interruptible(poll_value * 2);

 	return 0;
